1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:45,360
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard, and with me today, RevenueCat CEO Jacob Iting.

7
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:50,080
Our guest today is Taylor Wells, Director of Data Products at NewsCorp.

8
00:00:50,400 --> 00:01:02,320
Having worked on data projects at Deloitte, Disney, and Business Insider, Taylor now works on data strategy, product insights, and identity frameworks across NewsCorp's many digital products.

9
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:16,160
On the podcast, we talk with Taylor about how to make better decisions with data, the many pitfalls of collecting and interpreting data, and why the best executive dashboardard is probably a handwritten weekly email.

10
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:19,600
Hey Taylor, thank you so much for joining us on the podcast today.

11
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:20,480
Thanks for having me.

12
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:21,280
Excited to be here.

13
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:28,960
So you've had a ton of experience working on data at Deloitte, Business Insider, Disney Plus, and now News Corp.

14
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:35,760
And I wanted to kick things off just talking about like what data science is.

15
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:36,640
Why data?

16
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:39,440
I would almost separate that into two different things.

17
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:45,680
Why I choose to beat myself up getting into a career in data or why data matters.

18
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:56,480
Honestly, on the why data matters part, I would almost start with, instead of looking at it as what data can do for you, it's more just like, how badly data can be misused, frankly, these days.

19
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:58,560
I think that you see that across most companies.

20
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:21,600
So everyone, when they think about how data and analytics and data science teams work, if they're not in those spaces but are in corporate settings, they often think of the problems that they've observed in the past, broken reports or problems trying to get to data or a lot of time, effort and money spent on data related initiatives or things like that that ultimately don't show much of the value that they think that it's going to show.

21
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:29,360
So I think it's very why I think it's important that people to be hesitant or reluctant about the return on investment in data itself.

22
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:39,680
But I think that people also realize that data as a concept and as a raw set is essentially some of the most valuable assets or products on Earth when used well.

23
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,280
So it's almost like atomic energy or something like that, right?

24
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:48,640
Like, it can be used for great things, but you remember Fukushima and things like that instead.

25
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,800
So everyone knows what bad data looks like and the challenges that you have.

26
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:59,840
Like they start a new initiative where they're going to build out a new data warehouse or move something over and then that turns into a slog.

27
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:04,240
There are now changes that were unexpected or it's even harder to do something.

28
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:07,760
It takes longer than they thought or costs are crazy out of control.

29
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:15,600
And then you also have things like companies that go out and hire a lot of data scientists or data analysts, but they don't really know what they should be focused on.

30
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:23,440
And it's more an afterthought of like, I'm going to hire great talented, smart people in those spaces because I don't understand them.

31
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:25,520
And they'll figure it out as we're going.

32
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:29,280
And that can work, but it so often doesn't.

33
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:57,120
I think that when data is driven by a business strategy that informs a data strategy, then things are incredibleibly effective and you're able to really extract a lot of value and learnings out of data, especially in data science, in understanding consumer interaction and engagement in ways that are not privacy violating or like invasive, but are used to better that product and that experience for the customers.

34
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:03,120
Nobody wants a lot of ads on the page, but they may need to generate that ad revenue in order to support things.

35
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:21,200
But if you're actually making the experience better in order to have those ads on the page that are less invasive or the customer is not having to cover as much of that cost for the company because you've built things really effectively and intuitively internally, then it can ultimately add value across the board.

36
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:24,720
That's how I see data and data science adding value generally.

37
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:30,800
So then what would you say is the foundation of a good data practice?

38
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:36,240
Like data is just a bunch of raw numbers.

39
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:36,720
Right.

40
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:44,160
Well, one thing I would say is that more often than not, companies start by saying we should just be collecting everything and then we'll figure out what we need, right?

41
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:46,000
So there's a lot of challenges around that.

42
00:04:46,640 --> 00:05:00,720
But there's also the challenge of even if you have a honed focus on what you want to collect, say I don't need to record a viewport of the user, but I need to know at least when they click on a tile or whether or not the video plays whenever they click on the play button.

43
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:16,560
Without having structure around how you expect that to change over time to grow other use cases that are likely to come, you inevitably fall into the trap of building something that will have to be reversed or modified or adjusted.

44
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:20,000
And each step of that exponentially increases your cost.

45
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:32,080
Catching something early on could be one x of your cost and then ten x if you're fixing it by cleaning up downstream because now you've got new data coming in that you'd have to continually add new iterative processes to fix.

46
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:41,120
And then lastly, at the last mile, it may be 100 x more cost if you have bad reporting that are actually telling the wrong things because you have all this total frame.

47
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:43,200
Yeah, make a business move.

48
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:48,240
It's basically like when you look at data, data should be looked at in the same way that you're engineering a building.

49
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:53,920
You're going through the process of permits and agreements and alignment across the board on what things are going to be called.

50
00:05:54,320 --> 00:06:00,400
Are we going to call this a wall or are we calling like the wires within the wall like the conduit or are we calling it this?

51
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:04,880
so that if someone says that term later, we all know exactly what that is referring to?

52
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:22,720
So it's a lot of getting that alignment and then carefully laying out what you expect, both the current version and the potential modularity or general generic nature that it needs to be in, in order for you to continue to scale that out or use it for different use cases.

53
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:36,800
And then lastly, you need to really have a deep understanding from the data team side of what the business is trying to do even further than what they're asking for today, but also what the product does itself and then what your output looks like for the user.

54
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:42,880
You often see people that will build dashboards, but they don't necessarily understand how the dashboard is going to be consumed.

55
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:44,960
They just know the generic requirements of the dash.

56
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:51,360
And therefore, the dash may technically meet the requirements, but is a challenge to use or misses the mark on what you're trying to do.

57
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:52,480
How do we look at it?

58
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:52,800
Exactly.

59
00:06:53,600 --> 00:07:08,880
And on the flip side of that, you may have data engineers or data scientists who are trying to understand behavior based on how that's been described to them in the problem statements or the requirements, the tickets, whatever it is, but they are not regular users of the application and therefore don't understand how that works.

60
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:12,000
And it doesn't necessarily even mean that just heavy usage will fix that.

61
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:31,920
Like on Disney Plus, if you're a heavy user but you only have an Apple TV and you're saying, this is the behavior that I understand, which is very different than a low end Roku device or a phone and how they're engaging with the phone, like a swipe all the way down to the last row versus having to use the remote to do that, then you still may not fully understand how the product is being consumed, right?

62
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:41,520
So that deep understanding of what the upstream is, what needs to come out of this, and what are the business teams within it trying to accomplish both now and in the future.

63
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:57,120
Yeah, I think we see similar problems all the way from like indie developers with just the basic Firebased analytics., just collecting a ton of data, mislabeling that data, and then making bad decisions based on data they don't fully understand.

64
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:59,680
But then that propagates up at every stage.

65
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:11,360
Like you move from Firebase to Amplitude with the same mistakes, and then you move from Amplitude to like a custom data stack, make the same mistakes, and you have an entire data team propagating the same data mistakes.

66
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:18,480
What are some concrete things you've seen in what data to collect and how to think about that data collection?

67
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:22,400
I'm curious on that spectrum of collecting everything versus things that are semantically important.

68
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:24,720
Obviously, like I think both extremes don't work.

69
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:27,440
But collecting everything has cost implications, right?

70
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:31,200
And you're just drowning your data team in noise, typically.

71
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:33,680
So, like, how do you navigate that spectrum?

72
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:34,160
Right.

73
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:49,200
And that, frankly, I think one of the more hidden problems, too, is that when you have all that data, it's similar to saying, what are the lunch options and giving them two options to choose from, or three at a meeting versus bringing in an entire catering staff that can make any food.

74
00:08:49,520 --> 00:08:53,600
Now you've opened the door to like, well, I didn't actually want you to order an omelet.

75
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:58,000
We don't have the equipment for an omelet, but now you've ordered it because I've said that you could do that, right?

76
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:06,560
As well as whether meaningful, nonharharmful, but ultimately like violating data or willfully violating data privacy and usage.

77
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:22,400
You know, if you're collecting all data and listing it, for example, in the EU by their GDPR constraints as functionally necessary because it's an internal tool that you also use to make sure that the site is up, that the paywall is working, that consent is being captured, et cetera.

78
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:35,200
And you're flagging those so they can't be used downstream, but you're not gate that to where it technically can't be used downstream as well, or masking it or protecting it, or omitting it when not needed, then you now open the door for a potential risk.

79
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:51,680
So I think that the balance is to go back to the original question of like where is the right mix?

80
00:09:52,160 --> 00:10:02,800
If there was a clear answer to that for an app versus a website versus a cell phone or whatever you're manufacturing, then it would be more standardized across the industry.

81
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:08,400
But the fact is it really goes back to what is the company trying to accomplish.

82
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:15,760
I think that even in our earlier talks we talked about how data in itself is a not a value added to the business.

83
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:19,920
Collecting it, storing it, having that is not a value to the business.

84
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:39,360
It's whether or not you're actually using it and turning it into meaningful insights that impact the business, not even insights just for the sake of insights, which is also a problem area where you get kind of that 90% of the way, but it's not actually tied to the decision makers or the right levers aren't available for those people.

85
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:45,040
So I think it is literally a process of not trial and error, but a full understanding across data.

86
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:51,120
And that's why to kind of your example that you gave earlier of how it just continues to work the problem up in different ways.

87
00:10:51,680 --> 00:11:06,800
If you're starting without a foundation, every time you restart and wondering why it's not working, it's because you have to slow down and set that foundation, have a full understanding across those teams, and then everything else becomes exponentially faster and easier.

88
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:10,240
But so often companies don't do that.

89
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:24,880
And frankly, you know, I get to brag or hang my hat on the fact that Disney Plus was not only such a great success, which a lot of that is a mixture of things happening, COVID and things like that, but it was also a great luxury to start from scratch.

90
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:38,000
from a data perspective on an app that we had the full support of the company, both financially and backing it and pushing it as this is what our future will look like.

91
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:47,360
This will be a key factor as much as parks or anything else, but also that it was they gave us the runway to launch the app a year and a half.

92
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:54,640
Now you see these streaming apps come out after like two months of being announced because they can kind of burn through that, but that opens the door for a lot of problems too.

93
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:58,560
Or they try to bolt several together and then they're building on legacy systems.

94
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:02,240
With Disney Plus, we were starting from scratchratch, ESPN plus, Disney plus.

95
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:05,600
We got to really go in and say, what do we need to collect?

96
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:08,160
How is this going to work across all the devices?

97
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:19,840
What are the limitations of each device that we need to factor in so that we can normalize some of this information and ensure that we aren't tracking something in one way on one and not that's what I mean when I say semantics.

98
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:24,960
When you're coming up with a tracking plan, it's like thinking about the semantics and how can you compare that?

99
00:12:25,280 --> 00:12:31,920
Because to some degree you don't want to compare an action on a phone from an action on an OTT device, but in some cases you do.

100
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,160
Probably like a watch should mean the same thing.

101
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:34,480
Yes.

102
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:35,680
Right, to some degree.

103
00:12:36,320 --> 00:13:14,880
made the point of like some amount of trial and error and guesswork and that's kind of what I've found historically is like for indies and whatever like when you're thinking about that first iteration of what do I care about just be logical go through and use your app and think about the things you do that you think would be interesting to know and put those 10 or 20 actions in and that's a good place to start and then I've always found it's usually if you miss something it doesn't take long to go back and add you know you might lose some historical data but also you keep making the point that data as collected is in some ways valueless right like it's like crude oil or some other raw asset like until you're refining it and actually action on it, it actually has zero value.

104
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:19,840
So don't worry too much about hoarding everything, because I've seen people do that too.

105
00:13:20,320 --> 00:13:26,480
And like it can be used, but as you said, like there's implications for data regulation and privacy.

106
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:34,560
There's also implications for costs, like running a data, if you have your own data warehouse or even just running through all of these events platforms, they usually charge on events.

107
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:42,160
Some of them charge on users, which is nice, because then like if you have a user, you can track zero or one hundred thousand events, which is nice, but they're not all like that.

108
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:45,200
And so, yeah, just being somewhat logical is a good start.

109
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:48,880
Yeah, to that point, I think that there are ways to structure how you're approaching it.

110
00:13:48,880 --> 00:14:04,240
So you can imagine how you're approaching it in a generic form where you can ultimately not then hinder changes going forward where it has changed like what you're trying to collect by having that framework that actually allows for modularity or scale.

111
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:14,720
So one example would be if you were planning to track how many people clicked a download button in a streaming app to see how many people actually use the download feature, how many downloads are completed.

112
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:22,560
You might build an event that is download clicked, download started, download paused, download completed, deleted, whatever.

113
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:52,320
If you're focusing generically, you can say, I'm going to build tracking for button clicked or click action, and then give keys to those buttons, ensure that there's structure in how that's built, so there's a bottom button that has to sit within a container that sits within a set, we have a page, and I've got those hierarchical keys built up so that now when someone comes back and says, Well, now we need to know when they hit download and then pause and then share and then download again, oh, that's a new function.

114
00:14:52,640 --> 00:15:00,720
Well, now I can just say that either way, those were clicks of those actions, and I've mapped that behind the scenes to what those keys and tags represent.

115
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:11,760
And that's kind of like how you ultimately can say, we don't necessarily need to know everything upfront, but we haven't backed ourselves into a corner where we were tracking something as download click before and now it's button click.

116
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,640
And I've got to merge that data and clean it and normalize it.

117
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:16,480
Which you can do that pretty easily.

118
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:23,360
I mean, that's still pretty far down the semantic meaning stack versus like tap on x value, y value.

119
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:27,920
Which you can theoretically could collect what does every single tap event on the device?

120
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:33,680
And that's probably pretty useless, but hooking into your generic button class and making sure you capture all those.

121
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:36,240
I think about some of the tracking plan for RevenueCat.

122
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:56,240
for revenue cat is like we did have semantic stuff like created an app, created a project, which just creates, you know, going to the conversation about downstream consumers, if you have an event that is like project created that sets you up for much better self-service use cases, because most people understand what that means and they can probably pull something together and it most likely will be correct, but we still also have a page loaded event, right?

123
00:15:56,480 --> 00:16:08,000
So like we know when a page loads and we have the path broken down and in some cases we've been able to retroactively, without going in and reengineering a semantically tracked whatever, we're able to go in and retrofit that in, which is good.

124
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:10,880
And that's still like, you know, there's not that many page loads, right?

125
00:16:11,520 --> 00:16:12,880
There's not that many button clicks, right?

126
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:13,200
So you don't have that many button clicks right now.

127
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:17,920
And honestly, as things mature over time, you really figure out what you need to be focused on.

128
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:24,320
For example, do I need to know that all the icons of the home menu were shown when they clicked the word home?

129
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:31,760
I can assume fairly safely with QA testing and things like that of the app that all nine tabs are showing.

130
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:34,640
I don't need to confirm that nine were there every time.

131
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:38,960
But to your point, some of that can also be done within the stream of data.

132
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:45,360
And you can say that this button with this type that's being clicked has a key that represents that.

133
00:16:45,600 --> 00:16:59,200
And as that data is being pulled in, I'm enriching it to then classify it as this is project started or app created and send that to the teams that it's relevant to while still being able to reverse engineer the raw data that you collected before.

134
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:06,960
But where companies, I think most people think of GA, Google Analytics and others when they think of these things is it collect, especially Adobe.

135
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:16,000
Each event is collecting a hundred pieces of metadata across them, yet they're almost all identical for that session or that user or whatever.

136
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:23,920
And in broad ways, like for a lot of the interactions, offloading some of that to essentially just capture when any of those states change.

137
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:33,840
And then being able to apply logic downstream to how frequently they're resetting a password instead of just saying I'm capturing password field every event.

138
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:35,200
Don't recommend that one.

139
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:36,960
Don't capture your password fields.

140
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:38,000
Officials, horse.

141
00:17:38,080 --> 00:17:38,320
Example.

142
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:52,480
So once you start collecting data, one of the things you and I talked about before the podcast was how data is really just a symbolic representation, but that information is what we derive from the data.

143
00:17:53,360 --> 00:17:56,640
So tell me a little bit more about what you meant by that.

144
00:17:57,040 --> 00:18:00,560
Yeah, so I think everyone also knows that's had a data set anywhere.

145
00:18:01,040 --> 00:18:07,120
that executives will often hone in on something and then there's not a lot of questions asked as to whether or not it's still needed.

146
00:18:07,280 --> 00:18:08,320
Is it tracking the right thing?

147
00:18:08,560 --> 00:18:09,360
Are we looking at the right thing?

148
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,520
And are we making any decisions out of it?

149
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:22,560
I can go into a theoretical company every week and give a weekly status that shows how many subscriber signups there were across the app or how many page views or whatever.

150
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:27,520
But the question is rarely asked, are we doing something with this information each time?

151
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:28,400
And if so, what?

152
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,160
Are we retroactively doing it?

153
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:31,440
Do we do it every week?

154
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:33,200
Would we like to do it more frequently?

155
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:34,800
Are we able to do it more frequently?

156
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:36,960
And so that's kind of the value that ultimately comes from the app.

157
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:40,320
the value that ultimately eludes companies around data very often.

158
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:42,400
What do you think the bottleneck is there?

159
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:47,280
Because like you have ostensibly smart people consuming this, but like there's failure to act.

160
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:51,520
Is that because there aren't things to do, which I think is maybe even a valid path?

161
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:53,280
What's your theory for when that fails?

162
00:18:53,520 --> 00:18:56,000
Yeah, I think it depends on the company size, frankly.

163
00:18:56,320 --> 00:19:02,960
So at a larger company, what you often get is people not asking questions because a senior asked for it and they don't want to push back.

164
00:19:03,200 --> 00:19:12,560
And so you're building layers in between to the point that the people that are actually executing on building a report or surfacing a report or interpreting a report can't even.

165
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:14,480
necessarily get that question.

166
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:16,080
That tells me it's a stupid question.

167
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:18,480
Just that would save me so much time.

168
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:21,680
Because that's actually probably what the crux of what I'm trying to get to.

169
00:19:21,840 --> 00:19:23,120
I have a curiosity about something.

170
00:19:23,680 --> 00:19:25,600
And so I ask, oh, can we pull this together?

171
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:31,520
If someone who knows better than me of why this data is trash could tell me before I spend all that time.

172
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:36,000
Or knows what you'll ask after you get there and just gets you what you want.

173
00:19:36,400 --> 00:19:36,880
What I want.

174
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:39,360
Like knows the why of what I'm trying to ask.

175
00:19:39,440 --> 00:19:39,920
But you're right.

176
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:45,040
And I'm sure it depends on the culture you have as a company as to how much pushback you can give and stuff like that.

177
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:45,520
Right.

178
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:48,000
And again, I think a lot of these are rooted in some foundational things.

179
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:54,720
We're trying to answer things like do the employees of the company across the board fully understand what the business is trying to do?

180
00:19:55,040 --> 00:20:01,680
I've worked at too many companies where it is unclear to all how it all comes together.

181
00:20:02,160 --> 00:20:04,000
What is ad tech and marketing doing?

182
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:06,160
What are the business development teams doing?

183
00:20:06,480 --> 00:20:08,080
What is strategy focusing on?

184
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:09,600
Who are our core customers?

185
00:20:09,920 --> 00:20:17,840
Even at a prior publication, I found it incredible to see that when asked what their audience was, who are your readers?

186
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:23,920
Who are the people that you're trying to reach, but like who are your readers today, that they would have strong opinions of who those are.

187
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:28,000
But they were based on the assumptions of the executives, right?

188
00:20:28,320 --> 00:20:29,680
Like maybe it's even the CEO.

189
00:20:30,160 --> 00:20:34,240
Our readers are tech savvy, Northeastern or West Coast.

190
00:20:34,720 --> 00:20:37,040
They don't want old legacy media, blah, blah, blah.

191
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:41,200
Like whatever the definition is, yet you go back and say, what is this based on?

192
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:44,240
Are these based on personas that you've created from data science?

193
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:49,120
Is this customer interviews and like working sessions for how the product is actually consumed?

194
00:20:49,280 --> 00:20:50,240
Have you done polls?

195
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:54,320
Do you send out newsletters and you see what they're responding to and then you're profiling it?

196
00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:55,600
It's often no.

197
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,400
It's just like that's what I built it for originally.

198
00:20:58,560 --> 00:21:00,320
And I'm assuming that that's who my audience is.

199
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:02,000
audiences because no one said otherwise.

200
00:21:02,240 --> 00:21:13,920
So it's really just like breaking that down, like if no one's pushing back to say we can't define our customer based on nothing, based on just our assumptions, then things continue to only actually like exponentially do that.

201
00:21:14,160 --> 00:21:23,280
Who do you think are like for data teams to understand what the business should be focusing on and like how do you think the best way for those folks to get that information, where should that come from?

202
00:21:23,520 --> 00:21:23,920
Absolutely.

203
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:26,560
It should be a coupling with data product, frankly.

204
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:28,800
Data product is that weird space, right?

205
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:31,520
It is not a product team in the traditional sense.

206
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:33,520
And I like to think of myself as a product person.

207
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:35,600
Can you define data product a little bit?

208
00:21:35,840 --> 00:21:36,320
Yeah, exactly.

209
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:38,800
But data product kind of lives in between.

210
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:41,040
I've seen it live under product and engineering.

211
00:21:41,120 --> 00:21:42,400
I've seen it live solely under product.

212
00:21:42,720 --> 00:21:47,200
I've seen it live under its own umbrella under data, like chief data officer potentially.

213
00:21:47,520 --> 00:22:00,480
And then I've seen it live in some hybrid forms or embedded models like a hub and spoke, where you have different people from the data product side working closer with the businesses while other product teams also are involved.

214
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:05,280
So think you're developing a new feature like 3D video for a streamer.

215
00:22:05,680 --> 00:22:10,720
You may have that team that's conceptualizing the 3D video that they've pitched and sold executives on.

216
00:22:11,040 --> 00:22:47,680
You have a data product person or team that are working now with the engineers alongside that to understand what that product team envisions for that, how they actually expect it to happen, how engineers actually expect that it can be built and delivered in order to inform how they should collect and capture that data and how it's going to impact all the stuff that they actually sit on top downstream, and then they have to take that message and understanding back to the data science, data engineering and data analytics teams to explain that, sell the vision of what we're trying to track, get feedback on how it could be better done or how they envision that it's most efficiently done.

217
00:22:48,400 --> 00:22:49,840
What are some pitfalls, et cetera?

218
00:22:50,240 --> 00:22:56,800
And then lay out that roadmap of features, you know, like in a JIRA sense, you know, like the work that needs to actually happen.

219
00:22:57,200 --> 00:23:08,560
So you're saying, like, the people in the company who are responsible for integrating data into the tip of the spear, product features and stuff like that, they're learning through osmosis or direct interaction with the product leaders.

220
00:23:08,880 --> 00:23:09,040
Right.

221
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:13,840
And the engineering team is building, who should ideally have a better understanding of why they're doing it.

222
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:19,760
And that often would not be effective to try and ask them to understand the data part while going on that.

223
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:21,200
In fact, it often never works.

224
00:23:21,360 --> 00:23:24,400
If you were to go to a product team and say, put data first in your mind.

225
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:44,240
when you're developing a new product idea or a new feature, they will still end up, even if they're coming up with KPIs and metrics that they're going to track to measure ROI and success down the road, they still end up going, Oh, I forgot about that because I was so focused on seeing the feature come together in wireframes, tests, blah, blah, that now it's live and we don't have any data for it.

226
00:23:44,320 --> 00:23:53,680
Now I've actually seen that even at a publication doing a podcast or streaming audio where they'll partner with a third party company, build this thing or this idea.

227
00:23:54,080 --> 00:24:03,200
We all know data is important and that we'll need to understand the data about it, but at the last second you're like, Now this has actually already been pushed out live and we're not collecting any data on it.

228
00:24:03,520 --> 00:24:03,920
Oh no.

229
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:04,880
Well, what do we do?

230
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:06,720
How do we do it a little bit before that?

231
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:08,960
Usually I do my data tracking like in the last day.

232
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:09,600
Right.

233
00:24:10,080 --> 00:24:18,640
Which honestly kind of is fine because like you don't know and the instrumentation these days is at least for the like indicator using like a tool or something like that can be done pretty quickly.

234
00:24:18,800 --> 00:24:21,840
But yeah, I also you said teams coming up with KPIs.

235
00:24:21,920 --> 00:24:22,480
It's interesting.

236
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:33,840
I've watched us build subfeatures and things like this that we design very bespoke and interesting KPIs for and a KPI's usefulness goes down proportional to the amount of time you spend thinking about it.

237
00:24:35,600 --> 00:24:40,160
Like the best ones are just like how many people use, how many dollars through features.

238
00:24:40,400 --> 00:24:47,840
And I almost wonder if going back to interpretability, it's like if a KPI, you need a PhD to design it, you probably need at least a master's to understand it.

239
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:51,040
And let's be frank, like most people don't understand that stuff.

240
00:24:51,200 --> 00:24:52,320
So don't overthink it.

241
00:24:52,960 --> 00:24:55,520
You know, like in fact, don't overthink it might be.

242
00:24:55,920 --> 00:24:58,880
Yes, don't overthink it as it starts to grow.

243
00:25:00,640 --> 00:25:07,920
A lot of my examples too here are companies at large scale that are like, I was going to say most of these are a lot of investments in advance.

244
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,800
In terms of like, oh, you have a data product person who's bringing stuff back.

245
00:25:10,880 --> 00:25:12,640
And like most companies, it's either going to be, well.

246
00:25:12,720 --> 00:25:16,240
well, startups, it's going to be the founder, maybe like the lead engineer.

247
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:21,040
Maybe if you're a little bit big, you've got someone who's like data literate, but they're going to be going across the stack.

248
00:25:21,200 --> 00:25:26,480
They're probably responsible for the technical stack, but then also probably the instrumentation and like all that stuff.

249
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:34,480
And I've been that guy before where I've had to be like, jumping up and down, being like, hey, like, you know, we don't have to do a lot, but like, let's make sure we collect the basics here.

250
00:25:34,880 --> 00:25:42,000
The beauty is, I think, today there's enough tools that you can get eighty percent of what you need with some of this off the shelf stuff.

251
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:51,920
But yeah, I mean, even at RevenueCat, like Dave and I were talking about build versus buy on the way over here in terms of like, even just like an event tracking platform and we're like, yeah, I don't know why anyone would ever build internally.

252
00:25:52,160 --> 00:25:56,000
And I was like, well, actually today we're almost kind of building our own internally.

253
00:25:56,480 --> 00:25:57,120
Like, we are.

254
00:25:57,440 --> 00:26:06,160
Because at some point you begin to rely on like usually an internal BI system and you probably have a data warehouse and at that point it's like, well, tracking event streams is kind of trivial at that point, right?

255
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:08,960
And there's some customization advantages you can get.

256
00:26:09,200 --> 00:26:24,000
Yeah, I'm really curious to hear your thoughts on that because you and I were talking about your time at Disney Plus where it was SERP era, there were high hopes for Disney Plus and there was almost a blank check for what you were able to build inside Disney Plus.

257
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:33,200
And I'm curious, you know, it sounds like it was ultimately very successful, being able to ground up, but very few companies have the luxury.

258
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:45,600
I think you told me at one point there was like three hundred engineers and multiple teams across the company all working on essentially building an entire analytics company inside Disney.

259
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:49,840
And that was really not even outside of Disney Direct to Consumer.

260
00:26:50,160 --> 00:26:52,880
That's not taking into account parks, cruises.

261
00:26:53,120 --> 00:26:53,200
Yeah.

262
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:54,400
So yeah.

263
00:26:54,480 --> 00:27:08,400
So then what are your thoughts on the build versus buy and blending the two and when it actually star makes sense to start building out your own data product versus relying more on off the shelf, what are the benefits and drawbacks of each?

264
00:27:08,880 --> 00:27:15,120
And then after hundreds of engineers building this, now you still need tens of engineers to maintain it over time.

265
00:27:15,840 --> 00:27:22,800
Very few companies, especially in our space in subscription apps, have the luxury of dedicating teams to that.

266
00:27:23,040 --> 00:27:29,360
And then if they do, maybe those ten engineers would even maybe be dollar dollar RD spend.

267
00:27:29,520 --> 00:27:31,280
Like it's almost doesn't really make sense.

268
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:38,240
Yeah, like their time would be better spent on consumer facing features or just like building infrastructure.

269
00:27:38,560 --> 00:27:40,960
So I've, yeah, really teed up a long question.

270
00:27:41,520 --> 00:27:42,000
No, no, no.

271
00:27:42,080 --> 00:27:46,000
I think this is actually going to be one of the more succinct ones for me.

272
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:56,240
I think it does depend, but I would generally lean towards use off the shelf, use open source, things like that early on if you're not expecting immediate massive scale.

273
00:27:56,560 --> 00:28:33,120
Unless you just think that you've struck the motherload in terms of an idea for an application or website and you could, there is a 10% chance that you could suddenly have 100 million users or something and now you backed yourself into a vendor corner from a volume perspective or pricing, then start with something more basic, but with a clear evaluation structure around when you cut over to other things, and a culture that both acknowledges and accepts that it's okay that things may not be congruent over time when you switch.

274
00:28:33,680 --> 00:29:02,000
One of the biggest pitfalls of every company that's existed for more than a couple of years that I've been at is literally that they say, well, we've already started collecting this, and if we cut over to something new, it won't match the old reports or I can't end up with these like if we start these ideological layers of like I mean imagine the panic that's been going across the industry in publishing where it's not tech focused for the most part about the switch from GA 360 to GA 4.

275
00:29:02,560 --> 00:29:20,880
GA 360 lived on for years and years past when it should have when Google said that they would kill it because customers were just so concerned about my bosses are saying that this is a completely new way to do it and they're not ready to start looking at things from a session perspective versus an event perspective or a device perspective.

276
00:29:21,200 --> 00:29:24,000
It's a sea change in the way that we track it and we know that it's better.

277
00:29:24,800 --> 00:29:29,200
But it means that now I can't show year over year comparisons that actually have a lot of value.

278
00:29:29,280 --> 00:29:32,720
Or like the charts that we've relied on for years are now like invalid.

279
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:34,720
They just don't make sense in this new paradigm.

280
00:29:34,880 --> 00:29:34,960
Yeah.

281
00:29:35,200 --> 00:29:35,920
It's very painful.

282
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:37,040
Organizational change.

283
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:38,240
Yeah, absolutely.

284
00:29:38,400 --> 00:29:44,720
I mean, even think about something like for Disney Plus, the idea of the bundle was introduced very late in the lead up to the launch.

285
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:50,960
We had an idea, again, we, I'm kind of just referring more to like non executive levels that something like that would happen.

286
00:29:51,200 --> 00:29:56,880
But we also thought that it was more likely that it would just become subsumed in some way in either direction.

287
00:29:57,040 --> 00:30:01,120
Like maybe it's Disney Plus merging into Hulu or vice versa, but that you end up with one of app.

288
00:30:01,360 --> 00:30:02,800
And I still think that will ultimately happen.

289
00:30:03,200 --> 00:30:12,160
But at the time, the idea of needing to synchronize user access across Hulu and Disney when someone changes to a bundle option, that wasn't present.

290
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:13,680
The idea of ads wasn't present.

291
00:30:13,920 --> 00:30:18,080
So, like, you didn't really have the ability to figure out how that was then going to look on a report.

292
00:30:18,320 --> 00:30:26,480
And it kind of like, as long as the company culture was setting the expectation of, like, I don't actually care if it translates over time, then you can win.

293
00:30:26,720 --> 00:30:31,600
But also, again, startups have the luxury of saying, we don't have any historical data.

294
00:30:32,160 --> 00:30:38,640
So as long as we're moving quickly and we end up in a good spot in the first couple of years, then you can usually ass establish a good practice going forward.

295
00:30:39,040 --> 00:30:48,320
And when those businesses are coming back and trying to challenge like that, but I want it this way, just pushing back on what value do you get out of being able to translate that?

296
00:30:48,640 --> 00:31:06,880
If they have all historical cliques of an article for the Journal or Business Insider going back for ten years so that they can show a line graph of ten year month over month growth down to like a section, but the sections have changed over time and now they're frustrated that the reports are not going to be congruent.

297
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:12,160
Say, what value are you getting in comparing something that happened ten years ago when the market was absolutely.

298
00:31:12,480 --> 00:31:13,520
Oh, it's a renewed chart.

299
00:31:14,240 --> 00:31:15,280
It's just nice to look at charts.

300
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:15,680
Exactly.

301
00:31:15,840 --> 00:31:16,400
Yeah, yeah.

302
00:31:16,480 --> 00:31:17,040
What's wrong with that?

303
00:31:17,200 --> 00:31:17,840
But they're not pushing.

304
00:31:18,080 --> 00:31:18,880
Is that not a valid example?

305
00:31:20,880 --> 00:31:24,160
They're often not pushing back on the boss to say, yeah, yeah.

306
00:31:24,480 --> 00:31:26,720
We actually, like, what are we going to do if we see a big change?

307
00:31:27,200 --> 00:31:27,600
I don't know.

308
00:31:27,760 --> 00:31:28,480
It's like a photo album.

309
00:31:28,640 --> 00:31:31,760
I just like to be able to flip through and be like, oh, remember when we were at this much?

310
00:31:32,080 --> 00:31:33,280
But, you know, it's a really good point.

311
00:31:33,440 --> 00:31:45,600
I mean, I think about our transition from off the shelf amplitude to like, you know, when did we add an ETL or like, when did we add a phase where we could like transform some of the, like, evented data into something more semantically meaningful?

312
00:31:46,240 --> 00:32:40,800
And yeah, it kind of happened when we started to break definitions and not just break them through like foolishness but through like okay the app is different now like I think one big example for us was like we sort of refactored what it meant to be a platform versus like different platforms anyway big structural change to how we did stuff and suddenly like X did not mean X anymore right and like to encapsulate that sometimes in a third party tool like amplitude it can be very difficult because like the operator of building charts and stuff has to have that information constantly loaded in their memory or they'll pull up stuff that doesn't make sense it's like non-congruent right and that's where for us it made sense to introduce some sort of pipelining step because then what we can do is encode that change into a pipeline and be like, okay, like in this month of this year, we change this definition and create some sort of like normalized super definition that takes some of the sharp edges off for data consumers.

313
00:32:41,120 --> 00:32:43,840
But again, if you're just launching your app, like you don't need that.

314
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:45,920
Like that is a luxury of a problem to have.

315
00:32:46,080 --> 00:32:50,160
And to your point earlier, you're not going to launch that app that has 100 million users in the first year.

316
00:32:50,480 --> 00:32:55,040
And anyway, if you do and you owe Amplify a bunch of money, you don't care.

317
00:32:55,360 --> 00:32:56,000
You're like, great.

318
00:32:56,400 --> 00:32:58,400
Like one fire, I don't have to worry about right now.

319
00:32:58,560 --> 00:33:00,000
They'll cut you a deal, probably, right?

320
00:33:00,240 --> 00:33:00,560
Absolutely.

321
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:08,000
So, to that point though, like at scale where it can be problematic if you do have success is, you know, when we built Glimps internally.

322
00:33:08,400 --> 00:33:08,960
And Glimps is.

323
00:33:09,280 --> 00:33:18,000
Glimps is the internal event structure of click stream data within Disney Plus, which is still there today, at least since the last time I looked.

324
00:33:18,480 --> 00:33:20,720
So, those events were that generic structure.

325
00:33:21,040 --> 00:33:22,000
It was named Glimps.

326
00:33:22,240 --> 00:33:26,880
It's an acronym for gathering live interactions via multi purpose surfacing of events.

327
00:33:27,040 --> 00:33:29,040
Whichever in that meeting, someone felt really proud.

328
00:33:29,360 --> 00:33:29,680
That was me.

329
00:33:31,040 --> 00:33:32,960
And I had come up with about forty of them.

330
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:34,000
Like, what?

331
00:33:34,320 --> 00:33:36,000
One night of, like, what are we going to call this?

332
00:33:36,640 --> 00:33:38,240
And by the way, there's a reason I did it.

333
00:33:38,480 --> 00:33:41,760
It's because my boss had been calling it UAT for so long.

334
00:33:42,240 --> 00:33:43,440
User activity tracking.

335
00:33:43,600 --> 00:33:47,040
And I was saying, UAT means user acceptance testing.

336
00:33:47,200 --> 00:33:48,400
We can't confuse it.

337
00:33:48,720 --> 00:33:49,760
You have to call it something else.

338
00:33:49,920 --> 00:33:52,240
So I finally said, I have to come up with an acronym.

339
00:33:52,320 --> 00:33:56,800
So you came in pitching like a director, like pitching a new movie, you're like storeboarding.

340
00:33:58,400 --> 00:33:59,520
It was a rapid slack dump.

341
00:34:00,080 --> 00:34:04,160
And my boss at the time, the head of data, Laura Evans, she's fantastic.

342
00:34:04,400 --> 00:34:05,600
She just said, I don't care.

343
00:34:05,840 --> 00:34:06,480
Glimpsed data.

344
00:34:07,520 --> 00:34:09,200
Like, I had sent a big list of them.

345
00:34:09,520 --> 00:34:10,400
Oh, you know they care.

346
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:11,360
That's impressive.

347
00:34:11,600 --> 00:34:16,240
Pre GPT for being able to come up with such a fancy acronym.

348
00:34:16,480 --> 00:34:23,200
Oh, it would have saved me countless nights while my two year-olds were sleeping and should have been getting a bedtime story.

349
00:34:23,680 --> 00:34:26,160
Countless nights of me coming up with ridiculous acronyms.

350
00:34:26,320 --> 00:34:31,120
I mean, we were joking about it, but naming and interpretability of this data really matters.

351
00:34:31,360 --> 00:34:32,160
Like it really matters.

352
00:34:32,560 --> 00:34:35,360
I mean, even a cute name for a system helps.

353
00:34:36,080 --> 00:34:37,600
It helps people talk about a thing.

354
00:34:37,760 --> 00:34:48,400
And like we, I'm battling this now in some cases in terms of data, a lot of times in terms of how we explain the product, and it's not always like, hey, at a high level it's easy to explain our product, but like a specific aspect of our product.

355
00:34:49,040 --> 00:34:55,440
Like you need a surprising number of names for things and the consequences of choosing those names is a lot higher than you think.

356
00:34:56,880 --> 00:35:02,400
I mean, I think design, UI, product marketing, all that are so underrated, frankly, these days.

357
00:35:02,640 --> 00:35:16,080
Like just because it's AI is the focus and people want to cut costs and that's often an area that they feel like they can cut costs because now every app has been built like in several different ways, but there are not many like new ways that people are coming up with things.

358
00:35:16,160 --> 00:35:20,320
So they think, oh, I could just use like standard practices and such and not have to worry about it.

359
00:35:20,480 --> 00:35:22,240
But branding is incredibly important.

360
00:35:22,480 --> 00:35:36,400
Think about Apple intelligence and how much we'll be saying that term for the next five to ten years, right, if it's successful versus if they just stuck with, we added AI in here, because no one will continue to call Google's services with Gemini.

361
00:35:36,960 --> 00:35:39,600
It'll more for whatever, or they'll just never say it.

362
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:45,600
They'll just say, it's Google with AI, right, but to brand that in a way that Windows did with Copilot, right?

363
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:50,880
Now you see that ecosystem expanding to now their hardware is as a Copilot driven laptop.

364
00:35:51,200 --> 00:36:00,160
So I think that's so important, but it also then, like you said, allows people internally to discuss things quickly without having to go back to like, where is this?

