00:37:17,360 --> 00:37:20,240
You have to maintain the DPAs and make sure all that stuff's up to date.

385
00:37:20,320 --> 00:37:25,440
Like GDPR really, I mean, I actually think the requirement for disclosure of third party data processing was good.

386
00:37:25,520 --> 00:37:30,800
I think it was one of the things that came out of GDPR that was really pretty well done and like maybe could have been more simplified.

387
00:37:31,120 --> 00:37:32,880
I feel like there's a lot of DPA theater.

388
00:37:33,280 --> 00:37:37,840
This is like very much like analytics company problems, but like people just need a DPA.

389
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:38,880
It's like, all right, okay.

390
00:37:39,120 --> 00:37:45,200
You could also just tell your customers you're sending data to us and what you're sending and everybody will just trust everybody, but I guess we'll have to do this little dance.

391
00:37:45,520 --> 00:37:46,000
I get it now.

392
00:37:46,320 --> 00:37:48,800
And like the nice thing too is like the tools are rolling.

393
00:37:49,200 --> 00:38:00,720
some of this stuff yourself are becoming, you know, between the data, you know, just talking about AWS, like the ways for you to like store bulk data in AWS, the tools on processing over large flat files has gotten so much better.

394
00:38:01,200 --> 00:38:08,160
Tools like DBT have made it much better to like manage like SQL pipelines, and then the database options have gotten so much better too.

395
00:38:08,320 --> 00:38:10,080
Like when we Snowflake.

396
00:38:10,160 --> 00:38:11,760
Yeah, yeah, Snowflake has changed everything.

397
00:38:12,320 --> 00:38:16,160
Even Redshift has come a long way from where it was when we started using it a decade ago.

398
00:38:16,560 --> 00:38:19,200
Clickhouse I haven't used personally, but there are like options.

399
00:38:19,440 --> 00:38:20,240
Yeah, right?

400
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:25,040
And so I still think like when you're at day zero, don't do any of that that stuff.

